# {{ title }}

## Introduction {#sec:intro}

Markdown was released in 2004 [@gruber2004] and has many dialects today.[^dialects]
YAMD keeps its spirit but fixes its rough edges.[^1]

## Footnotes {#sec:footnotes}

Footnotes are numbered in the order they are first used, whatever their labels.[^zebra] The label `zebra` comes first here, so it is note 3.[^apple]

You can use the same footnote twice.[^dialects]

Short notes can go inline.^[Inline notes don't need a label or a definition.]

A footnote can have several paragraphs.[^long]

[^dialects]: CommonMark, GitHub Flavored Markdown, MultiMarkdown and Pandoc are the best known.
[^1]: Numbers work as labels too.
[^zebra]: This label sorts last alphabetically, but it is numbered by use.
[^apple]: This one is numbered after `zebra`.
[^long]: The first paragraph of a longer note.

    Indent following paragraphs to keep them in the note.

## Citations {#sec:citations}

Cite one source [@lamport1978], several at once [@lamport1978; @dean2008], or a specific page [@knuth1984, p. 47].
The reference list appears where you put `::bibliography`, or at the end.

## Cross-references

Label figures, tables, equations and sections, then refer to them with `[@label]`:

- [@sec:intro] introduces the topic.
- [@sec:citations] covers citations.

$$ {#eq:pythagoras}
a^2 + b^2 = c^2
$$

[@eq:pythagoras] is numbered automatically.

